# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) if you need to override anything.
# For the laptop workflow you do not: every value below has a default
# that is correct there.

# Step 11.1.2 — where the frontend looks for the backend.
#
# Unset, api.ts derives it from wherever the page itself was loaded
# (`window.location.hostname:8000`), which is right on a laptop: the
# phone reaches the backend over the same LAN address it loaded the page
# from, with no hardcoded IP.
#
# Hosted, that default is WRONG — nothing listens on port 8000 there. The
# deployed setup sets this to the EMPTY STRING, which means SAME ORIGIN:
# requests become relative (`/api/scan`), and CloudFront routes /api/* to
# API Gateway -> Lambda. One origin, so there is no CORS anywhere.
#
# The empty string is a real value here, not "unset" — api.ts checks
# `!== undefined` precisely so that `VITE_API_BASE=""` is honoured instead
# of falling through to hostname:8000. deploy.sh sets it this way:
#
#   VITE_API_BASE="" npm run build
#
# Set it to an explicit URL only if you deliberately split the frontend and
# backend onto different hosts — which reintroduces CORS and means the
# backend needs ALLOWED_ORIGINS set to match.
#
# VITE_API_BASE=
